message.content:
{
  "summary": "This bill proposes changes to the Individualized Education Act regarding who qualifies as an eligible student and what constitutes a participating school. It expands the definition of an eligible student to include Tennessee residents who are entitled to attend public school but have a medical condition that prevents them from attending in person, as determined by the state board of education. For students with this medical condition, a home school would be considered a participating school. 

For other students to be eligible, they must have a specific disability defined by the state board of education and an active individualized education program (IEP) listing that disability as primary or secondary. These students must also have been enrolled in a Tennessee public school for the previous full year, be enrolling in a Tennessee school for the first time, or have received an individualized education account (IEA) in the previous year.

Parents of students with qualifying medical conditions must use IEA funds only for approved expenses. The department would be required to monitor these funds for compliance and may suspend or permanently terminate a student's participation in the program if the parent fails to follow these rules. The bill would take effect on July 1, 2026."
}
